Exploring Python Libraries: tls_client vs curl_cffi for Web Requests
AI is becoming increasingly important in our lives and is impacting various aspects of society. It has also significantly influenced the world of web scraping. The aggressive data collection strategies employed by AI teams (as everyone seeks to gather more data to improve their models) have pushed websites to adopt stricter anti-bot protections. As a result, traditional libraries like scrapy and requests are no longer effective for a growing number of websites.
Today, I want to review two Python libraries: tls_client and curl_cffi. On GitHub, I found 848 repositories using curl_cffi and 83,423 using tls_client. Both libraries are designed to help bypass website bot protections. But what’s the difference between them? Let’s take a closer look.
Both Chrome and Safari 17.0 user-agent impersonations produce distinct fingerprints. The differences in JA3/JA3n/JA4/JA4_r/JA4_o and Akamai hashes, along with variations in the Akamai text, show that these two browsers have different SSL/TLS characteristics, even when both are configured to run on the same system and with similar configurations. This demonstrates how a simple change in user-agent (from Chrome to Safari) can significantly alter the fingerprint of an HTTP request, potentially bypassing bot protection mechanisms that rely on such fingerprints for detection.
